# Udemy Transcript + Textbook Documentation

When this rule is applied or the user provides a Udemy lecture URL (or has one open), do **all** of these every time:

1. Save the **transcript** to `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/notes/`
2. Capture video frames every **5–10 seconds**, keep useful figures, delete the rest
3. Write **textbook documentation** to `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/md/`
4. Add or update the lecture link on the landing page `Personal/Files/aws/01.md`

Use `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/sample.md` as the documentation style guide. Do **not** write lecture content into `01.md`. `01.md` is only the landing page + table of contents. Manual archive is `Personal/Files/aws/old.md`.

## A. Extract the transcript

1. Navigate to the lecture URL (`browser_navigate`) or use the open tab.
2. Snapshot the page (`browser_snapshot`).
3. Open the **Transcript** panel if it is closed (transcript toggle / `data-purpose="transcript-toggle"`).
4. Collect cue lines in order (`role: button` names between video controls and **Autoscroll**).
5. Get the lecture title and **sequence number** from course content (example: `Play 2. The SAA-C03 Exam` -> `2`).
6. Save `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/notes/<sequence>-<Hyphenated-Title>.txt`:

```
Lecture: <title>
URL: <url>
============================================================

<transcript lines>
```

Filename rules: spaces to `-`, keep readable Title-Case, drop special characters except hyphens.

## B. Capture figures every 5–10 seconds

Prefer **seeking** through the video at 5–10 second intervals (video time), not waiting in real time. Seeking is faster and still captures every illustration.

1. Wait until `video` is loaded (`readyState >= 2`, non-zero duration). Play once if needed, then mute and pause.
2. Create a temp folder if needed, e.g. `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/md/images/_tmp-<sequence>/`.
3. Loop `currentTime` from `0` to `duration` in **8-second** steps (use 5s for short/dense diagram videos, 10s for long talking-head).
4. After each seek, wait briefly for the frame, then `browser_take_screenshot`.
5. Save raw frames as `_tmp-<sequence>/tXXX.png` (XXX = seconds).
6. **Keep** a frame when it shows a new slide, diagram, table, console step, or important UI. Place keepers at `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/md/images/<sequence>-<short-slug>-figN.png`.
7. **Delete** black/unloaded frames, near-duplicates of the same slide, and leftover `_tmp-*` files after documentation is written.
8. If the player stays black, retry play/seek once. If still blocked, use the best available still and note that in the figure caption.
9. CodeSnap is for editor/code captures only. For Udemy slides/console, use browser screenshots.

Embed each kept figure next to the transcript content it illustrates, with **Figure N.** title and a short description.

## C. Write textbook documentation

Create `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/md/<sequence>-<Hyphenated-Title>.md` matching `sample.md`:

- Title heading with lecture number
- Course + lecture links + transcript path
- **Introduction** (prose a novice can follow)
- **Detailed Explanation** as numbered steps, with `- [x]` bullets from the transcript
- **Figures** beside the matching step
- Collapsible **Lab**, **Terminal Commands**, **Code**, **Questions and Answers**
- **Summary**
- **References** (Udemy lecture, official AWS docs, transcript file)

For HOL/console lessons, write a real step-by-step tutorial a beginner can repeat. For theory lessons, say there is no lab and keep Q&A exam-focused.

## D. Update the landing page

In `Personal/Files/aws/01.md`, add the new lecture under the correct section in **Table of contents**:

```
N. [Lecture Title](udemy/md/N-Hyphenated-Title.md)
```

Do not duplicate an existing TOC row. Do not put full lecture notes in `01.md`.

## Confirm

Tell the user: transcript path + line count, doc path, figures kept vs deleted, and that `01.md` was updated.

## Examples

- Transcript: `udemy/notes/1-Welcome-and-How-to-Use-this-Course.txt`
- Docs: `udemy/md/1-Welcome-and-How-to-Use-this-Course.md`
- Transcript: `udemy/notes/2-The-SAA-C03-Exam.txt`
- Docs: `udemy/md/2-The-SAA-C03-Exam.md`
